\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Vector-like couplings and assigned representations}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The [onsite Yukawa operator](fdip-011M) acts at the base of
each cell, by the identity on exterior directions and by
#{Y(\phi_x)} on the supplied fermion fiber. Its equivariance
assumption is a condition on the whole family,
#{Y(gv)=\rho_\psi(g)Y(v)\rho_\psi(g)^{-1}}. At a specified
scalar #{v} it gives commutation with the stabilizer of #{v}.
The bilinear of \ref{fdip-011P} uses one module and its dual,
as \ref{fdip-011W} explains. A given scalar weight must be
evaluated with its specified backgrounds and insertions.
The equal onsite determinants in \ref{fdip-018J} illustrate
the distinction. A separately weighted left or right sector
requires its own scalar-weight construction.}

\p{\citet{opening six-plet paragraph before (13), p. 657}{kobayashi1973cp}
assign three weak #{SU(2)} doublets to the left components of
their multiplet and #{6} singlets to its right components.
Those representation choices enter their weak-interaction
model. The finite group and the two trivial singlets in
\ref{fdip-018E} specify a different pair of actions, for which
one class already distinguishes the traces.}
